<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> Co-Prosperity Sphere was a concept proclaimed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> for a bloc of Asian nations under Japanese leadership, free of Western colonial rule. In practice it served to justify Japanese expansion and the economic exploitation of occupied territories during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It features in accounts of the Japanese imperial economy and its wartime resource needs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greater-east-asia-co-prosperity-sphere.jpg" alt="Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_1943_propaganda_booklet_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The idea of a unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> under Japanese leadership is traced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> in the 16th century, who proposed to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> one. On 3 November 1938 Prime Minister Fumimaro Konoe and Foreign Minister Hachirō Arita proposed the New Order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, limited to Japan, China and the puppet state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>, with six stated purposes that included joint defence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and economic co-operation. On 29 June 1940 Arita renamed the union the Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere in a radio address, and on 1 August Konoe extended its scope to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Japan also explained its expansion in Asia as analogous to the Monroe Doctrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The proposed objectives were economic self-sufficiency and co-operation among its members and resistance to Western imperialism and Soviet communism. In reality, militarists and nationalists saw it as a propaganda tool for enforcing Japanese hegemony, and Japanese spokesmen openly described it as a device for the development of the Japanese race. A secret document completed in 1943, An Investigation of Global Policy with the Yamato Race as Nucleus, laid out that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> would take the superior position. A Land Disposal Plan drawn up by the Ministry of War in December 1941, with the consent of Hideki Tōjō, envisaged both territories under direct Japanese administration and nominally independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states on the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The outbreak of war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the chance to conquer Southeast Asian territories for their raw materials, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> embargo on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> and oil it attacked British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonies in December 1941. Propaganda used phrases such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> for the Asiatics, and invading forces sometimes received warm welcomes, but the brutality that followed led many inhabitants to regard Japan as worse than their former rulers. Estimates put at about 100,000 the Burmese and Malay Indian labourers who died building the Burma-Siam Railway. Plans to use the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> for crops needed by Japanese industry failed, and Japan declared the Philippines an independent republic in October 1943.</p><p>The Greater East Asia Conference met in Tokyo on 5 and 6 November 1943, with Tōjō hosting the heads of state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and the Provisional Government of Free India and an envoy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. It was mainly propaganda, without practical plans for economic development or integration. The Sphere collapsed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender in September 1945. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ba-maw">Ba Maw</a>, wartime head of state of Burma, blamed the Japanese military for its failure, saying that it saw everything only from a Japanese perspective.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere</b>, the ideological framework of the wartime empire, promised Asian economic self-sufficiency but worked in practice as an extractive colonial system serving Japanese industrial and military requirements.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
